I've gotten to this section in the R-INLA book by Blangiardo.

Most models I'm running are like this -- "case-control" datasets of patients dichotomized based on a binary response variable, and my study question is whether the outcome is spatially heterogeneous.

To date the SPDE-INLA models I've run have all been using a single dataset, single stack, single projector matrixe. But this chapter suggests I should use a different procedure using a "joint bivariate" model.

The subsequent example in the book suggests that I:
 1) Make a single mesh and a single SPDE object
 2) Make TWO A objects, A_x and A_y (with I assume these being the two values of the response variable)
 3) Make TWO stacks. The y (response) variable for x would be y = c(x, NA) and for y it would be y = c(NA, x); then the stacks are combined.

I guess where I'm a little lost here is how this improves upon just running a single model with a single stack, in which the y variable takes on two possible values.

Again, to articulate my research question, I'm really testing whether this binary point pattern exhibits spatial clustering of high or low odds.

