Now, adding some comments specific to your problem.
Since you're not working globally, there are possibilities.

First, as you imply, you likely don't need daily timescale for the seasonal field; it should vary fairly smoothly over time, so quadratic b-spline basis function in time, with a few days or even a month between the knots should be ok.
This is the technique I use for the global problem as well, but unfortunately I don't have anything written in a form that's ready to be released outside the project right now. I do have slides from presentations about the general approach.

The basic space-time construction in the Cameletti et al paper might be helpful,
but you'll likely find that your daily timescale would require a ridiculous amount of memory if run as a single batch in inla.  There are options for using smaller inla runs as building blocks for the full solution, but that's ongoing research.

Finn

> On 21 Feb 2017, at 12:46, Mark R. Payne <mp...@aqua.dtu.dk> wrote:
>
> Hi,
>
> We are looking for an example of fitting a space-time climatology and anomaly fields with INLA.
>
> To explain what I mean by this, consider a large collection of sea surface temperature measurements spread across e.g the North Atlantic for many years. Temperature shows a strong spatial pattern and temporal variability on (at least) two time-scales - a seasonal cycle (winter vs summer) and also interannual variability (e.g. due to climate change)
>
> The model that I would like to fit is therefore of the style
>
> T ~ c(x,y,t1) + a(x,y,t2) + e
>
> where
> * T are the temperature  observations
> * c(x,y,t1) is a seasonal climatology, giving the average temperature as a function of the day of year (t1), and, looping on 1 Jan
> * a(x,y,t2) is the anomaly field, giving the temperature anomaly relative to the climatology for all of the time points (t2) in our data set.  e.g. from 1 Jan 1960 to 1 Jan 2017. The mean of this anomaly field should be zero.
>
> The time resolution of t1 and t2 need not be the same, and I suspect that computational limitations will probably mean that they are not.
>
> Does anyone know of any comparable examples that we could use to help us develop such a model?
>
> Thanks,
